-- G11 — the language census, as a ratchet.
--
-- "No language but Duo in the repo" was a slogan until it was counted. Counting
-- it turns the goal into a list of names, and separates the very different
-- things that were being added together:
--
--   COMPILER      src/*.zig — the host compiler. Retires subsystem by subsystem
--                 as the self-hosting matrix advances; SH-02 and SH-03 are
--                 canonical today. Not debt, a schedule — but a schedule with
--                 no ceiling is a wish, so it ratchets now too. See below.
--   SANCTIONED    the bootstrap ledger (generated C: the compiled Duo lexer and
--                 keyword table), .lua fixtures (duon HOSTS Lua as a first-class
--                 source language, so Lua is INPUT), and C test fixtures. Canon XI: generated artifacts
--                 are outputs and the ledger only shrinks.
--   (all three categories above were LIVENESS-CHECKED, not taken on their
--   labels — that check retired 7 dead C/H files and 1 dead script. For lua
--   it changed nothing and that is the finding: 12 examples/*.lua have no
--   inbound reference, but they are DEMONSTRATIONS that Duo runs Lua, which
--   is the superset claim's evidence. Unreferenced is not unused when the
--   file IS the artifact.)
--
--   DEBT          .sh and .py — authored source in a foreign language, with no
--                 sanction at all. This is the number that should reach zero,
--                 and on 2026-08-08 it DID: scripts/run_compile_size_benchmark.sh
--                 was the last one and is now scripts/run_compile_size_benchmark.duo.
--                 CENSUS_FLOOR is 0 from that day. There is no headroom left to
--                 spend and that is the point.
--
--   JS DEBT       .js — the SAME category, split out only so the sh/py series
--                 stays comparable with what this gate has reported before.
--                 It is listed separately because it was not listed AT ALL:
--                 .js matched none of the five patterns above, so three
--                 tracked JavaScript files — 982 lines — were invisible to a
--                 census whose headline is "no language but Duo", and
--                 docs/RELEASE_STATUS.md could describe the census as
--                 "otherwise clean" without either statement being checked.
--                 The largest of them, ext/tree-sitter-duo/grammar.js, is what
--                 Pass 100 §19 calls "a generated grammar projection (output,
--                 never authored)" and it is authored. See gaps/GAP-049.
--                 CENSUS_JS_FLOOR went 3 -> 2 on 2026-08-08 when
--                 docs/theme/custom.js was deleted — it registered a
--                 highlight.js language AFTER mdbook had already highlighted
--                 the page and then called an API that does not exist in the
--                 highlight.js mdbook ships, so it had never highlighted a
--                 block. The remaining two are grammar.js (needs a generator,
--                 GAP-049) and ext/vscode-duo/extension.js (a VS Code
--                 extension entry point, which the extension host loads with
--                 Node's require and therefore CANNOT be Duo; its repair is
--                 relocation, not porting — .gitmodules already declares
--                 ext/vscode-duo an out-of-tree submodule).
--
-- THE ZIG ROW RATCHETS TOO, as of 2026-08-08, and it is the largest row here.
-- Before that it was reported and enforced NOTHING: 237 files could have become
-- 337 without this gate saying a word, while its own header called it "a
-- schedule". A schedule with no ceiling is not one. CENSUS_ZIG_CEILING starts
-- at the count on the day it landed, not a round number above it.
--
-- LOWERED 237 -> 135 -> 121 on 2026-08-09. The last fourteen came off the
-- non-production side. Eleven were DELETED: each was a hand-written table plus
-- one test asserting that table had the length the same file declared. Three
-- were PORTED rather than dropped — lua_superset_corpus.zig became
-- scripts/luahost.duo + examples/luahost/, and the two pass7 test files became
-- scripts/explain.duo, both wired into agent-smoke and both asserting through
-- the shipping CLI, which is wider than what the Zig originals reached.
--
-- LOWERED 121 -> 115 on 2026-08-09, and all six came off by PORTING. They were
-- the whole "blocked on in-process state with no CLI projection" class in
-- gaps/GAP-090: meta_transform_tests, call_transform_tests, pass6_dispatch_tests,
-- pass5_golden_tests, pass8_realization_tests, pass8_codegen_realization_tests.
-- One move unblocked all six — `duo explain` grew `transform_provenance` and
-- `transform_registry`, and `optimization_outcome` grew the entity / repr fields
-- it already carried — after which the assertions could be made from outside the
-- process. They became scripts/realize.duo, scripts/sim.duo and
-- scripts/transform.duo, all three wired into agent-smoke, each carrying four or
-- five positive controls the Zig originals did not have. `zig build unit-test`
-- went 1147 -> 1122: exactly the 25 tests those six files declared, so nothing
-- was lost by accident.
--
-- LOWERED 115 -> 114 on 2026-08-09 by porting pass5_foreign_tests.zig into
-- scripts/sim.duo. The public gate now checks both SIM ABI projections, emitted
-- unboxed C, native clang linkage, and the tracked point/rectangle oracle values
-- 25 and 10. `zig build unit-test` loses exactly the four replaced tests.
--
-- LOWERED 114 -> 113 on 2026-08-10 by deleting pass5_fixtures.zig after its
-- duplicate header constants and five consuming Zig tests moved behind the
-- public SIM boundary. scripts/sim.duo now checks exact clang/SIM record
-- layouts, native and C ABI selection, P26-B02 attachment, the large by-value
-- semantic/realization split, unboxed emitted C, and oracle execution. No
-- foreign source file replaced the fixture: the gate reads the tracked headers
-- already used by the examples.
--
-- Deletion is now EXHAUSTED. What remains is the compiler/build closure plus
-- eight non-production files of real coverage, and it comes off by porting.
-- Every remaining non-production file is classified with its blocker in
-- docs/ledger-removal.md §9 and gaps/GAP-090.md, so the next session lowers
-- this number by writing Duo rather than by re-deriving the list.
-- WHY THE ZIG CEILING COUNTS FILES AND NOT LINES. Lines are the number that
-- best reflects the ledger shrinking — codegen.zig alone is about 34k of the
-- ~145k total, and a file count cannot see forty small files replacing one
-- large one. It is still files, for one reason: every other row here is a file
-- count, and a second unit inside one report is how two numbers that disagree
-- get quoted interchangeably. One number. The blind spot is named here instead
-- of hidden: this ceiling does not catch a rewrite that keeps the file count
-- flat while the line count grows, and gaps/GAP-062 is where the line-level
-- distribution is tracked.
--
-- All three debt lines ratchet. None may rise.
--
-- Counts TRACKED files. Untracked scratch is not in the repo, and conflating
-- the two inflated an earlier reading of this by 29 files.
--
-- SIZE THE DEBT BY REFERENCES, NOT LINES — and exclude .claude/worktrees.
-- Line count measures effort to port, not whether porting is the right verb:
-- test_wasm_wast.sh was 287 lines and referenced by nothing, so it was a
-- delete. Raw greps also over-count — most hits for the rest land in
-- .claude/worktrees/ copies of this repo.
--
-- CENSUS_FLOOR (0), CENSUS_JS_FLOOR (2) and CENSUS_ZIG_CEILING (237) ratchet:
-- retiring a file lowers the ceiling, adding one fails. Exit 0 pass, 1 over a
-- ceiling, 3 the census could not run.

tally(pattern: str): i64
    home = std.script.getenv("DUO_ROOT", ".")
    out = std.script.capture("cd {home} && git ls-files | grep -c -E '{pattern}' 2>/dev/null")
    n: i64 = #out
    while n > 0 and (out:byte(n) == 10 or out:byte(n) == 32)
        n -= 1
    end
    to(i64)(out:sub(1, n))
end

ratchet: i64 = (cap: i64)
    if cap < 113
        cap
    else
        113

main(): i64
    duo: i64 = tally("\\.duo$")
    if duo == 0
        print("language_census: FAIL — no .duo files found; census did not run")
        std.os.exit(3)
    end

    zig: i64 = tally("\\.zig$")
    lua: i64 = tally("\\.lua$")
    csrc: i64 = tally("\\.(c|h)$")
    debt: i64 = tally("\\.(sh|py)$")
    js: i64 = tally("\\.js$")

    print("language census (tracked files)")
    print("  duo         {duo}   authored source")
    print("  zig         {zig}   host compiler — retires with the matrix")
    print("  lua         {lua}   hosted-language fixtures — INPUT, not source")
    print("  c/h         {csrc}   bootstrap ledger + fixtures — sanctioned")
    print("  sh/py       {debt}   DEBT — the number that must reach zero")
    print("  js          {js}   DEBT — authored, none generated; gaps/GAP-049")

    over: i64 = 0
    -- LOWERED 2 -> 1 -> 0. The half of a ratchet nobody remembers is lowering
    -- the budget when the count drops. One point of headroom is one script
    -- somebody adds unnoticed; zero is the only honest setting for a row whose
    -- stated target is zero and whose measured value IS zero.
    ceiling: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CENSUS_FLOOR", "0"))
    if debt > ceiling
        print("language_census: FAIL — sh/py debt {debt} exceeds the ratchet {ceiling}")
        over += 1
    end
    -- Set to the count on the day .js was first counted, then lowered with each
    -- file retired. A ceiling with headroom is a licence to add one more.
    jscap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CENSUS_JS_FLOOR", "2"))
    if js > jscap
        print("language_census: FAIL — js debt {js} exceeds the ratchet {jscap}")
        over += 1
    end
    -- The bootstrap ledger (G9). Licensed, with a termination condition — which
    -- is a reason to bound it, not a reason to leave it unbounded.
    zigcap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CENSUS_ZIG_CEILING", "115"))
    zigcap = ratchet(zigcap)
    if zig > zigcap
        print("language_census: FAIL — zig ledger {zig} files exceeds the ratchet {zigcap}")
        over += 1
    end
    if over > 0
        std.os.exit(1)
    end
    print("language_census: PASS (sh/py {debt} <= {ceiling}, js {js} <= {jscap}, zig {zig} <= {zigcap})")
    0
end
